            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Mars Perseverance rover news is that NASA says the rover is studying a region called **“Broom Point”** on the rim of Jezero Crater, where it found evidence that a **245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rock** was built by **repeated asteroid impacts**. NASA describes the layered bedrock there as likely **more than 3.9 billion years old**, making it an important record of Mars’ early history【5:1†source】【5:2†source】.\n\nOther recent updates:\n\n- **New Broom Point imagery:** Perseverance returned a sweeping view looking back toward the outside lip of Jezero Crater’s rim, captured by its Mastcam-Z instrument【5:1†source】.\n- **Route to Broom Point:** NASA also released an orbital map showing the rover’s path to the Broom Point member, helping illustrate how the mission reached this ancient layered terrain【5:1†source】.\n- **“Marathon” milestone:** Perseverance recently completed the equivalent of a full marathon on Mars — **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** — reaching that distance on sol 1,890 of the mission, much faster than Opportunity did【5:0†source】.\n- **Orbital snapshot:** NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter/HiRISE captured Perseverance from above as a tiny speck on the surface, along with its tracks, just before it crossed the marathon-distance milestone【5:2†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is currently making headlines for both **major science at ancient impact-formed rocks near Broom Point** and a **historic driving-distance milestone** on Mars.",
